# Lecture 05F

## Degrees of Dominance

## Chapter 5

## Dr. Neil Voss

## Sept 29, 2026

=== layout: one-panel

# Learning objectives

@body

- Compare complete, incomplete, and codominance
- Predict phenotypes from monohybrid crosses
- Infer possible ABO genotypes
- Distinguish one-gene and multigene traits

@notes

Use the heterozygote as the comparison point. ABO and the donor-recipient examples make distinct allele effects visible; later examples contrast one-locus and multigene traits.

=== layout: section

# Patterns of dominance

@notes

Begin by comparing how the heterozygote looks under different patterns of dominance.
The next slides introduce the terms used for those patterns.

=== layout: one-panel

# Extending Mendelian Genetics for a Single Gene

@body

- Inheritance of characters by a single gene may deviate from simple Mendelian patterns in the following situations\:
  - When alleles are not completely dominant or recessive
  - When a gene has more than two alleles
  - When a gene produces multiple phenotypes

=== layout: one-panel

# Degrees of Dominance

@body

- Complete dominance: heterozygote matches dominant homozygote
- Incomplete dominance: heterozygote intermediate between homozygotes
- Codominance: both alleles expressed distinctly
- Polygenic inheritance: multiple genes; cumulative, continuous traits
- Epistasis: one gene masks or modifies another gene

=== layout: section

# Incomplete Dominance

## The phenotype of the heterozygote is a blend between the two parental phenotypes, showing a mix rather than one being dominant.

=== layout: one-panel

# Incomplete Dominance

@body

- The phenotype of the heterozygote is a blend or intermediate between the two parental phenotypes.
- Neither allele is completely dominant, resulting in a mixed or blended trait.
- Example\: In a specific variety of flower known as the four o&rsquo;clock plant, crossing red-flowered (RR) and white-flowered (WW) plants results in offspring with a light pink flower color (RW), illustrating blending rather than dominance.

=== layout: two-panels

# Incomplete dominance: F&#x2081; and F&#x2082;

@left

![Four-o-clock flowers](assets/lect05f-degrees_of_dominance/image_001.jpg)

- P: red C&#x1D3F;C&#x1D3F; x white C&#x1D42;C&#x1D42;
- F&#x2081;: all pink C&#x1D3F;C&#x1D42;
- F&#x2081; gametes: 1/2 C&#x1D3F;; 1/2 C&#x1D42;

@right

| Egg / sperm | C&#x1D3F; | C&#x1D42; |
| --- | --- | --- |
| C&#x1D3F; | C&#x1D3F;C&#x1D3F;: red | C&#x1D3F;C&#x1D42;: pink |
| C&#x1D42; | C&#x1D3F;C&#x1D42;: pink | C&#x1D42;C&#x1D42;: white |

=== layout: two-panels

# Incomplete Dominance

@left

![Slide image 2](assets/lect05f-degrees_of_dominance/image_002.jpg)

@right

- Incomplete dominance governs the human voice pitch, eye color, and hair curliness
  - the trait produced is an intermediate one\: instead of straight or curly hair, incomplete dominance leads to something in the middle&mdash;wavy hair

=== layout: two-panels


@left

![Slide image 3](assets/lect05f-degrees_of_dominance/image_003.jpg)

@right

- [Red versus white flower color in snapdragons shows incomplete dominance]{color=red}

- Incomplete dominance yields a 1\:2\:1 phenotypic ratio in the F&#x2082;
- The normal phenotype ratio observed in the F&#x2082; is 3\:1 for a simple dominant/recessive allele pair.

