=== layout: section

# Polygenic Inheritance

## The phenotype is influenced by multiple genes, resulting in a continuous range of traits rather than distinct categories, demonstrating a cumulative effect on expression.

=== layout: one-panel

# Polygenic Inheritance

@body

- [Monogenic inheritance]{color=red} refers to genetic control of a phenotype or trait by a single gene.
- [Digenic inheritance]{color=red} refers to the interaction of only two genes that affect the phenotypic expression of a single trait.
- Many traits are not determined so simply, and either involve the interaction of many genes
  - [Multifactorial inheritance]{color=red} results from the interaction of many genes and with environmental effects.
  - [Polygenic inheritance]{color=red} results from the interaction of many genes, but has no environmental effects.

=== layout: one-panel

# Polygenic Inheritance

@body

- Polygenic inheritance refers to a single inherited phenotypic trait that is controlled by two or more different genes.
  - polygenic traits may display a range of possible phenotypes, determined by a number of different genes and the interactions between them.
- Examples of Polygenic Inheritance
  - Skin Color
  - Human Height

