=== layout: section

# Summary

=== layout: one-panel

# Degrees of Dominance

@body

- Complete dominance: heterozygote matches dominant homozygote
- Incomplete dominance: heterozygote intermediate between homozygotes
- Codominance: both alleles expressed distinctly
- Polygenic inheritance: multiple genes; cumulative, continuous traits
- Epistasis: one gene masks or modifies another gene

=== layout: one-panel

# Degree of Dominances

@body

![Slide image 23](assets/lect05f-degrees_of_dominance/image_023.png)

=== layout: one-panel

# Summary: start with the heterozygote

@body

- Complete: heterozygote matches one homozygote
- Incomplete: intermediate heterozygote
- Codominant: both allele effects distinguishable
- Dominance changes phenotypes, not allele segregation

@notes

Ask students to classify a heterozygote before naming a dominance pattern. Alleles remain discrete even when the phenotype is intermediate. Under the usual Aa by Aa assumptions, genotypes remain 1:2:1.

